      "text_result": "I diagnosed and fixed the CI failure.\n\n## Root cause (bucket a \u2014 upstream change broke a Percona assumption)\n\nThe `build-and-test` job reported \"1 fails to build\". The failing target was `wiredtiger_kv_engine_encryption_key_test`, with a cascade of errors all stemming from one ambiguity:\n\n```\nerror: reference to 'KeyState' is ambiguous\n  note: candidates are: 'enum class mongo::KeyState'          (index_bounds.h:209)\n  note:                  'enum class mongo::encryption::KeyState'  (key_state.h:39)\n```\n\nUpstream **SERVER-131801** (\"Move enums out of IndexBoundsChecker\") promoted `KeyState` from a nested `IndexBoundsChecker::KeyState` to a namespace-scope `mongo::KeyState`. The Percona test file does `using namespace encryption;` inside its anonymous namespace, so unqualified `KeyState` suddenly matched both the new `mongo::KeyState` and Percona's `mongo::encryption::KeyState` \u2014 making the `FakeKmipServer` class fail to compile, which in turn produced all the downstream `_keyStates not declared` / `no member readKey/getKeyState` errors.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdded a single disambiguating alias in the test's anonymous namespace:\n\n```cpp\nnamespace {\nusing namespace encryption;\nusing KeyState = encryption::KeyState;   // shadows the namespace-level mongo::KeyState\n```\n\nA direct declaration in the anonymous namespace takes precedence over the names injected at `mongo` scope, so every unqualified `KeyState` resolves back to `encryption::KeyState`. This adapts Percona code to upstream's rename rather than re-introducing a removed symbol.\n\nI verified this was the only affected site: the other two files with `using namespace encryption` reference `BadKeyState` (unrelated) or no KeyState at all, and files already inside `namespace mongo::encryption` resolve the nearer enum without ambiguity.",
